    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we must first identify the numbers the first die can roll that are less than 3. The possible outcomes that are less than 3 are 1 and 2.\n\nTo determine the number of possible outcomes that result in a number greater than 3, we must first identify all the possible outcomes the second die can roll. The possible outcomes on a single six-sided die are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6.\n\nOf these 6 possible outcomes, only 1 and 2 are numbers greater than 3. So, there are 2 possible outcomes that will result in the desired results.\n\nTo determine the total number of possible outcomes, we must identify the number of possible outcomes each die can roll. Each die has 6 possible outcomes, so the total number of possible outcomes is 6 x 6, or 36.\n\nTo determine the probability, we must divide the number of desired outcomes by the total number of possible outcomes. Thus, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than 3 on the first die and a number greater than 3 on the second die is:\n\n2 / 36\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{18}}",
