    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the probability that Nathan rolls a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Determine the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die.\n2. Determine the probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die.\n3. Multiply these probabilities together to find the probability of both events happening simultaneously.\n\n**Step 1: Probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die.**\n\nA six-sided die has six faces, numbered 1 through 6. The numbers less than three are 1 and 2. Therefore, there are 2 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n\nThe probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die is:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die.**\n\nThe numbers greater than three on a six-sided die are 4, 5, and 6. Therefore, there are 3 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n\nThe probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Combined probability of both events happening.**\n\nTo find the probability of both events happening simultaneously (i.e., rolling a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die), we multiply the probabilities of each individual event:\n\\[\n\\text{Combined probability} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1 \\times 1}{3 \\times 2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]",
